  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened workplace compliance with International Labour Standards in the educational and urban collective transport sectors and for migrants by 2025</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ILO is working closely with national and local partners in Brazil to strengthen compliance with international labour standards, promote occupational safety and health (OSH), and advance social justice, with a focus on vulnerable workers.

In Manaus, support to the urban public transport union is improving collective bargaining and social dialogue capacities, contributing to better working conditions. The Organization is also supporting the development of a socio-economic program for the population of Santo Ant&#xf4;nio de Jesus, a city in the state of Bahia, in response to a ruling by the Inter-American Court of Human Rights, reinforcing the role of labour standards in remedy and prevention.

Through a partnership with the Public Labor Prosecutor&#x2019;s Office (MPT), the Ministry of Labour and Employment (MTE), and the Ministry of Education (MEC), the ILO is advancing a national OSH initiative in schools to foster a culture of prevention and safer working environments, in line with Output 6.1.

The ILO has contributed to the launch of Brazil&#x2019;s National Migration Policy, supporting the creation of a network of welcoming cities to protect migrant workers, particularly women and those in informal employment.

Following the floods in Rio Grande do Sul, ILO technical inputs to a joint Damage and Loss Assessment (DaLA) are guiding government-led reconstruction efforts, with a focus on employment recovery in key sectors.

These initiatives contribute to SDG 8, particularly targets 8.6, 8.7, and 8.8, and emphasize inclusive growth, equality, and decent work for all.</narrative>
  </description>
